# Module Entitlement and Navigation

Frontend module and permission checks are for navigation and user experience.

Backend middleware and RBAC remain the security authority. Do not treat frontend
gating as sufficient authorization.

## Single registration

`features/registry.js` is the only place a feature declares itself. One entry
supplies the route, the sidebar placement, the tenant module, and the RBAC
resource:

```js
{ id: "brands", path: "/main/brands", section: "master-data",
  labelKey: "navBrands", icon: TrademarkOutlined,
  module: "master_data", permission: "brands", quickCreate: true },
```

Everything derives from it:

- the sidebar tree, via `buildMainNavItems` in `shell/sidebar/main-nav.js`
- `moduleForPath` and `permissionResourceForPath`, used by `ModuleRouteGuard`
- `ROUTES`
- the header quick-create menu

Do not add path-to-module or path-to-permission tables anywhere else. They used to
live in `lib/tenant-modules.js` and `lib/permissions.js` and drifted out of sync
with the sidebar; that is the failure mode this registry exists to prevent.

`lib/permissions.js` still owns the matrix itself — `usePermissions`, `can`,
`matrixAllows`. Only the path mapping moved.

## Registry fields worth knowing

- `nav: false` — routable but absent from the sidebar (profile, notifications).
- `gateNav: false` — the route guard enforces `permission`, the sidebar does not.
  Do not use this for settings: those leaves are gated in both places.
- `groupKey` — sub-heading inside the section panel.
- `leaf: true` on a section — the rail row navigates directly instead of opening a panel.

Every rail row and page row is distinguished only by its icon, so icons must be unique.

## Verification

`npm run check:registry` diffs the derived navigation and gating against a snapshot
of the reviewed behaviour. Run it after touching the registry.

When you intentionally change navigation or access, update the snapshot in
`scripts/check-registry.mjs` in the same commit — that diff is the review record
for a permissions change.

## Module codes

Use backend module codes exactly: `core`, `master_data`, `inventory`, `sales`,
`purchasing`. Do not invent frontend-only module names.

## Denied-access behaviour

`ModuleRouteGuard` notifies the user and redirects to the overview page. It fails
closed: a fetch error or empty matrix denies access.

Follow that existing behaviour rather than adding per-page checks.
